  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Bob wanted to tell his son the password of his CodeChef account, but as there are many people present in the surroundings, Bob encodes the password. Bob’s original password consists of digits 1,2,1, 2,1,2, and 333 only.\n\nTo encode the password, Bob decided:\n\n\nInstead of the digit 111, he would say ab\\texttt{ab}ab.\nInstead of the digit 222, he would say aba\\texttt{aba}aba.\nInstead of the digit 333, he would say a\\texttt{a}a.\nDue to noise, Bob's son did not receive the exact encoded password.\n\nInstead, he received a string SSS where the encoded password is a subsequence of string SSS and is uniquely decodable.\n\nNote that a string consisting of characters `a` and `b` is said to be uniquely decodable if it can be formed by encoding **exactly one** sequence of digits 1,2,1, 2,1,2, and 333.\n\nFor example, the strings `a` (formed by 333), `ab` (formed by 111), `aaab` (formed by 331331331) are uniquely decodable while `b` (cannot be formed), `aba` (can be formed by both 222 and 131313) are not uniquely decodable.\n\nBob's son wants to know the number of uniquely decodable subsequences in string SSS. As the answer can be very large, print it modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of one line of input which is string SSS.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the number of uniquely decodable subsequences of the given string modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤2⋅1051 \\leq T \\leq 2 \\cdot 10^51≤T≤2⋅105",
      "1≤∣S∣≤2⋅1051 \\leq |S| \\leq 2 \\cdot 10^51≤∣S∣≤2⋅105",
      "It is guaranteed that sum of ∣S∣|S|∣S∣ over all test cases ≤2⋅105\\leq 2 \\cdot 10^5≤2⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
